

Hi everyone.

I created this Confluence page to document the complete process we followed for the WIP/VIP certificate setup.

I will quickly go through the steps.


1. WIP AND VIP FLOW

First, this diagram shows our application flow.

Client or APIGEE
→ WIP
→ VIP
→ ECS/OpenShift
→ Application.

APIGEE knows our WIP name.

WIP selects one of the VIPs.

The benefit of VIP is that if our backend or cluster changes in the future, we can update it behind the VIP.

We don't need to ask APIGEE or other consumers to change their URL every time.

For example:

Today:
WIP → VIP → Old backend

After migration:
WIP → Same VIP → New backend

So this gives us flexibility when the backend changes.


2. CREATE CERTIFICATE THROUGH CMP

The next step is creating the certificate.

We raise a request in CMP.

I added the CMP link here and also added a sample questionnaire showing what details we provided.

This will be useful when we need to create or renew a certificate in the future.


3. FILES RECEIVED FROM CMP

Once the CMP request is approved, we receive an email.

The email contains three files:

Certificate file,
Private key,
and PFX file.

The certificate is the public certificate.

The private key belongs to that certificate.

And the PFX contains the certificate and private key together.

For our CAS setup, we used the certificate and private key.


4. DECRYPT PRIVATE KEY

The private key we received was protected with a password.

Before adding it in CAS, we needed to decrypt it.

We downloaded the files and removed the .txt extension.

Then we used Git Bash and this OpenSSL command.

[Point to command]

When we run the command, it asks for the password that we used while creating the CMP request.

After entering the password, it creates:

PrivateKey_decrypted.key

You can see the generated file in this screenshot.

We use this decrypted key in the next step.


5. ADD CERTIFICATE IN CAS

Next, we go to the CAS portal.

From here we go to:

Cloud Management
→ Network - Load Balancers.

Then we find our load balancer and open the required virtual server.

Under Certificate Management, we add:

the certificate content

and

the decrypted private key.

Then we submit the request.

After this is completed, our certificate becomes available in the application profile.


6. CHANGE VIP PROFILE

This screenshot shows our existing configuration.

Previously:

Application Profile Type was TCP.

Then we use Edit Application Profile.

We change the profile from:

TCP to HTTPS.

We select the required SSL option shown here.

Then the certificate that we added in the previous step appears under Service Certificates.

We select our certificate and submit the request.

So adding the certificate and selecting it in the profile are two separate steps.


7. FINAL VALIDATION

After the CAS request is completed, we test the application using the WIP URL.

We can test using SoapUI, Postman or curl.

We confirm that HTTPS is working and that we can successfully reach the application.


So overall, the process is:

CMP request
→ Receive certificate files
→ Decrypt private key
→ Add certificate and key in CAS
→ Change profile from TCP to HTTPS
→ Select the certificate
→ Submit
→ Test through WIP.

I documented the screenshots and steps here so we can follow the same process in the future for certificate renewal or similar certificate changes.

That's all from my side.









NURAREC – WIP/VIP CERTIFICATE CREATION AND CAS CONFIGURATION GUIDE

======================================================================
1. PURPOSE
======================================================================

This document explains the end-to-end process followed for configuring the HTTPS certificate for the NuraRec service.

It covers:

1. Creating/requesting the certificate through CMP.
2. Files received/generated from CMP.
3. Decrypting the private key.
4. Preparing the certificate and private key for CAS.
5. Adding the certificate in the CAS portal.
6. Configuring the certificate/profile against the VIP.
7. Required VIP/profile configuration changes.
8. Understanding the WIP → VIP → ECS/Application flow.
9. Validation after configuration.
10. Issues encountered and lessons learned.

IMPORTANT:
Do not include private-key contents, private-key passwords, keystore passwords, or other credentials in this Confluence page.


======================================================================
2. HIGH-LEVEL ARCHITECTURE
======================================================================

The application is accessed using the WIP hostname.

High-level flow:

Client / APIGEE
      |
      | HTTPS
      v
WIP / DNS-GSLB
      |
      | Selects/returns the appropriate VIP
      v
VIP 1 / VIP 2
      |
      | Network traffic forwarded to backend
      v
ECS / OpenShift
      |
      v
Service
      |
      v
Application Pods


WIP:
WIP is the externally known endpoint/DNS-GSLB layer. APIGEE/client uses the WIP hostname.

VIP:
The VIP provides a stable virtual IP. The actual backend/cluster destination behind the VIP can be changed without requiring upstream systems to change their endpoint.

For example:

Before migration:

WIP
 |
 v
VIP
 |
 v
Old Backend / Cluster

After migration:

WIP
 |
 v
Same VIP
 |
 v
New Backend / Cluster

This allows backend migration without changing the endpoint known to upstream consumers.

Inside ECS/OpenShift/Kubernetes, the service/router provides another level of routing/load distribution to the application pods.


