======================================================================
15. IMPORTANT TLS TERMINATION FINDING
======================================================================

During troubleshooting, connecting to the VIP showed an ECS/OpenShift wildcard certificate rather than the expected NuraRec WIP certificate.

A certificate similar to the following was observed:

*.apps.<ECS-domain>

This is an important architectural finding.

It indicates that traffic reaches the ECS/OpenShift ingress/router and that the certificate being presented may be coming from the ingress/router rather than directly from the application.

Also, testing an IP address directly does not provide the same TLS SNI/HTTP Host information as testing the real hostname.

Therefore, an IP-only test can return:

- Default wildcard certificate
- HTTP 503/default-route response

This alone does NOT prove that the NuraRec hostname configuration is incorrect.


======================================================================
16. CORRECT SSL VALIDATION
======================================================================

The certificate must be validated using the actual hostname/SNI.

Example:

openssl s_client -connect <VIP-IP>:443 -servername <NURAREC-WIP-HOSTNAME>


This tells the TLS endpoint:

"I am connecting for this NuraRec hostname."

Review the returned certificate:

- Subject
- SAN
- Issuer
- Validity
- Certificate chain


Curl can also be used with hostname resolution:

curl -vk --resolve <NURAREC-WIP-HOSTNAME>:443:<VIP-IP> https://<NURAREC-WIP-HOSTNAME>/


This forces the hostname to the specified VIP while preserving:

- TLS SNI
- HTTPS hostname
- HTTP Host header


